patch(b+'disc-sets-functions.md',[
('A type is a set of values. A database table is a set of rows. A hash function is a function from keys to buckets.', 'A simple model treats a type as a set of values and a mathematical relation as a set of tuples. SQL tables can contain duplicate rows, and a hash table usually maps a key through a hash value to a bucket.'),
('which reads exactly like a Python comprehension', 'which resembles a Python comprehension (mathematical sets can also be infinite)'),
("Python's built-in `set` gives you all of these", "Python's built-in set supports union, intersection, difference and symmetric difference; complement needs an explicit universe, U - a"),
('This is why brute-force subset algorithms are O(2ⁿ): 20 items is about a million subsets, 40 items is about a trillion.', 'There are 2ⁿ subsets: about a million for 20 items and a trillion for 40. Runtime also includes work per subset. The shown construction scans n positions for each mask, taking Θ(n·2ⁿ) elementary operations under a unit-cost bit model, before accounting for formatting and printing.'),
('and an SQL `CROSS JOIN` literally computes the Cartesian product.', 'and an SQL CROSS JOIN combines every input-row occurrence with every occurrence on the other side, retaining duplicate multiplicities.'),
('SHA-256 maps arbitrary byte strings (an infinite set) to 2²⁵⁶ possible outputs.', 'Standard SHA-256 accepts messages shorter than 2⁶⁴ bits and produces one of 2²⁵⁶ outputs. Its finite input domain is still vastly larger than its output space.'),
('This explains why a lossy step anywhere in a pipeline makes the whole pipeline lossy: you cannot recover what an early step threw away.', 'If a stage merges two reachable states that arose from distinct valid inputs, later deterministic stages cannot recover that distinction without additional information. Non-injectivity only on states the pipeline never reaches need not lose information.'),
('injections A -> B      n(n-1)...(n-m+1)\nbijections A -> A      n!   (permutations)', 'injections A -> B      n!/(n-m)! if m<=n\n                      0 if m>n\nbijections A -> A      m!   (permutations)'),
('There are nᵐ functions because', 'Use the counting convention 0⁰ = 1 for the unique empty-domain function. There are nᵐ functions because'),
('A Python function that reads the clock or a global variable is not a function in this sense, because the same input can give different outputs. **Pure functions** match the maths, which is why they are easy to test, cache (memoise) and parallelise.', 'A routine whose result depends on changing hidden state may return different results for the same explicit arguments. Including the relevant state in the mathematical input gives a function again; reading an immutable constant does not itself violate determinism. Pure terminating routines fit the simple total-function model and can be easier to test, memoise and parallelise.'),
('**Base64, UTF-8, gzip**: lossless encodings are injective. JPEG and MP3 are not.', '**Base64, UTF-8, gzip**: with a fixed encoder configuration and appropriate valid input domain, lossless round trips require injectivity. Ordinary lossy JPEG and MP3 encoding merge distinct inputs; the JPEG family also includes lossless modes.'),
('**Primary keys**: the mapping from key to row must be injective, which is what a `UNIQUE` constraint enforces.', '**Primary keys**: projecting each row to its primary-key value uniquely identifies that row. A primary key combines uniqueness with non-nullness; a bare UNIQUE constraint can permit multiple nulls depending on the database and configuration.'),
('f(x) = x² is surjective onto [0, ∞) but not onto ℝ.', 'With domain ℝ, f(x) = x² is surjective onto [0, ∞) but not onto ℝ.'),
('Side effects and hidden state break the "same input, same output" rule.', 'Changing hidden state can break the same-explicit-input/same-result rule; an output side effect can violate purity even when the returned result is deterministic.'),
('union, intersection, difference and complement map directly to Python\'s `|`, `&`, `-` and `^` operators.', 'union, intersection, difference and symmetric difference map to Python\'s |, &, - and ^; complement is U - A for an explicit universe U.'),
('bitmasks enumerate subsets, which is why subset brute force is O(2ⁿ).', 'bitmasks identify 2ⁿ subsets; constructing each list explicitly costs Θ(n·2ⁿ) total elementary operations.')], 'Fix function-count variable, subset-enumeration complexity, finite SHA input domain, set operators, SQL uniqueness/bag semantics and purity scope.',src)
def sets(q):
 q[5]['options'][0]['explanation']='Truncation cannot remove the pigeonhole limit. A 32-bit output is also too small for cryptographic collision resistance: generic birthday search needs only about 2¹⁶ trials.'
 q[5]['options'][2]['text']='Use the reduction ID mod 2³²'
 q[5]['workedExample']=q[5]['workedExample'].replace('Real hash tables therefore always include collision handling (chaining or probing).', 'General-purpose hash tables need a collision policy, such as chaining or probing; a table tailored to a fixed key set can instead use perfect hashing.')
 q[7]['workedExample']=q[7]['workedExample'].replace("(With NULLs, SQL's three-valued logic adds wrinkles, which is why the question rules them out.)", 'The De Morgan rewrite also holds under SQL three-valued logic. With NULLs, UNKNOWN is not the same as FALSE, so a set-complement interpretation of filtered rows needs care.')
 q[10]['options'][0]['explanation']='On all integers, f(-3) = f(3) = 9. Squaring is injective on the non-negative integers, and also on the non-positive integers, but not on all of ℤ.'
 q[11]['prompt']='Assume a deterministic encoder and decoder satisfy decode(encode(x)) == x for every valid value x. View encode : Values → Bytes and decode : Bytes → Values as total mathematical functions on an accepted byte-string domain containing every encoded value. What follows?'
 q[11]['workedExample']=q[11]['workedExample'].replace('Bytes outside the image of encode can decode to anything (or be rejected)', 'Accepted byte strings outside the image of encode can decode to other valid values; rejected inputs lie outside this total decoder domain').replace('A property-based test (e.g. with Hypothesis) checking the round trip is therefore checking that your encoding is lossless.', 'Property-based tests supply evidence on the cases generated; they do not establish the universal premise unless the entire finite domain is exhaustively checked.')
questions(b+'disc-sets-functions.questions.json',sets,'Clarify truncated-hash security, SQL NULL behavior, squaring domain and total decoder/testing assumptions.',src)
