patch(b+'disc-relations.md',[
('This is how Kruskal\'s algorithm tracks components, and how compilers unify type variables.', 'Union-find tracks components for Kruskal\'s algorithm and can support type-variable unification. Full type unification also needs to handle type structure and checks such as the occurs check.'),
("`==` must behave as an equivalence relation, and `__hash__` must be constant on each class (equal objects must have equal hashes), otherwise dicts and sets break.", 'Well-behaved value equality is normally an equivalence relation, but Python does not enforce these laws and NaN is a standard exception. Hashable objects that compare equal must have equal hashes, and their hash must stay stable while used as keys.'),
('Dependency graphs are usually drawn as strict orders.', 'An acyclic direct-dependency graph need not itself be transitive; its positive-length reachability relation is a strict partial order.'),
('CRDTs rely on a "merge" that is a join.', 'state-based CRDTs use a join operation on a semilattice of states; operation-based CRDTs use different delivery and commutativity conditions.'),
('(a cycle would violate antisymmetry)', '(a cycle of distinct vertices conflicts with antisymmetry of reachability, and a self-loop conflicts with strict precedence)'),
('Make, Bazel, pip\'s dependency installer, spreadsheet recalculation and SQL migration tools all do this.', 'Build and dependency tools use dependency ordering when prerequisites form a DAG. Cycle policies vary: pip documents an installation fallback for dependency cycles, so not every such tool rejects them.'),
('In a distributed database, each version carries a vector clock.', 'In a store that tracks versions with vector clocks, each version carries counters for its tracked participants.'),
('a R⁺ b if there is a path from a to b.', 'a R⁺ b if there is a path of positive length from a to b.'),
('For sparse graphs, a BFS or DFS from each node is usually faster.', 'With adjacency lists, a BFS or DFS from every vertex takes O(n(n+m)); this improves the asymptotic bound on sparse graphs, without promising a fixed wall-clock speedup.'),
('in a graph, it is "in the same connected component".', 'it groups vertices into connected components after ignoring any edge directions.'),
('A NaN key in a dict can only be found again by identity.', 'For ordinary float-NaN lookups, retaining the identical key object permits retrieval even though NaN is not equal to itself; another separately created float NaN does not match it.'),
('Timestamps from different machines give a total order that may not match causality; that\'s why Lamport and vector clocks exist.', 'Wall-clock timestamps can tie and need not match causality. Lamport timestamps respect causal precedence but do not detect concurrency; a process-ID tie-breaker can make them total. Vector clocks can expose concurrency under their model.')], 'Fix equality guarantees, direct-edge versus reachability orders, cycle handling, CRDT scope and clock distinctions.',src2)
