def counting(q):
 q[2]['options'][3]['explanation']='With independent uniformly distributed birth months, 5 people already have about a 61.8% chance of a shared month. This is not a guarantee; pigeonhole needs 13.'
 q[5]['options'][2]['explanation']='For these distinct inputs, 36 = 6² includes repeated positions such as (3, 3). itertools.permutations does not reuse an input position, though repeated values in its input can still appear more than once.'
 q[6]['options'][4]['explanation']='3 × C(10, 2) = 135, not 120. Picking a pair then a distinct third element would have 8 choices for that third element, and would count each triple three times.'
 q[9]['prompt']='A startup claims its lossless compressor, defined for all finite bit strings without an uncounted side channel, makes every 1 KiB (1,024-byte) file strictly smaller. Which statements are correct? Select all that apply.'
 q[9]['options'][3]['text']='For a lossless compressor defined on all finite bit strings, shrinking one input forces expansion of at least one other input'
 q[9]['workedExample']=q[9]['workedExample'].replace('1 KB', '1 KiB').replace('In practice, compressors like gzip and zstd fall back to storing incompressible input almost verbatim, so the output is slightly *larger*.', 'Concrete formats add framing or block overhead and can emit raw blocks, so incompressible input may grow. The theorem itself does not prescribe how a compressor represents those files.')
